using Gtk 4.0;

/**
 * The approval dialog's body: the rows, in the order a question needs them.
 *
 * What the tool is, what kind of thing it wants to do, where, and then the raw input in full.
 * `Adw.PreferencesGroup` would put every one of those in a list row with its own heading, which is a
 * settings screen around a question; a plain box reads as a question.
 *
 * **The structure and the geometry are here; the words and the `CSS.*` class names are not.** Every
 * string in this dialog is text an agent wrote, so it arrives from TypeScript — a template cannot
 * carry a label that changes per question. The class names are in `css.ts` as constants for the same
 * reason `APP_NAME` is in `constants.ts`: a `.blp` cannot import a TypeScript constant, and a literal
 * here would be a second place to keep in step with the stylesheet by hand.
 *
 * **`use-markup: false` on every label, without exception, and it is not the default.** `GtkLabel`'s
 * `use-markup` is TRUE in GTK 4, so a template that leaves it out renders agent text through Pango —
 * which would let `<b>allowed</b>` or a `<span>` smuggle formatting into a control whose button labels
 * are the decision. Every label here says it.
 *
 * **The locations row is declared hidden rather than left out.** Whether an agent named any paths is
 * a fact about the question, not about the widget, and one template has to serve both; TypeScript
 * shows the row when there is something in it.
 *
 * **`margin-start`/`margin-end` are widget properties here, and only here.** They exist in GTK 4 as
 * properties and *not* in its CSS, and `css.ts` records that the CSS parser rejects the logical
 * names while still loading the rest of the stylesheet — so a stylesheet using them looks like it
 * worked. A property in a template goes through GObject rather than the parser, which is what makes
 * the logical names safe on this side of the line.
 */
template $LotsePermissionBody: Gtk.Box {
  orientation: vertical;
  spacing: 12;
  margin-start: 6;
  margin-end: 6;

  // The header row: the kind's icon, the tool title (the strongest line in the dialog, not the loudest
  // one in the app), and the kind as a capsule at the end. The kind is metadata about the tool, and a
  // capsule gives it a place without the weight of a heading. `title-4` rather than `CSS.title`,
  // because that constant is `title-1` — the size of a *window* title, see `window.ts` on why the
  // sidebar bar does not use it. Literals, so unlike the `CSS.*` names they can live here.
  Gtk.Box {
    orientation: horizontal;
    spacing: 8;

    Gtk.Image kindIcon {
      pixel-size: 20;
      valign: center;
    }

    Gtk.Label titleLabel {
      use-markup: false;
      wrap: true;
      selectable: true;
      xalign: 0;
      hexpand: true;
      styles [
        "title-4",
      ]
    }

    Gtk.Label kindLabel {
      use-markup: false;
      valign: center;
    }
  }

  // One row for every path, newline-separated, so several locations read as one list instead of N
  // blocks — and so a path does not become the most prominent thing in the dialog. TypeScript either
  // fills this with the paths or replaces it with kurier's own "the agent did not say where", and
  // shows it either way.
  Gtk.Label locationsLabel {
    use-markup: false;
    wrap: true;
    selectable: true;
    xalign: 0;
    visible: false;
  }

  // The agent's own names for its options, as one caption line — and only when at least one of them
  // says something the kurier labels do not. Agent text, so `use-markup: false` like everything else
  // here; and dimmed, because it is metadata about the buttons rather than part of the question. This
  // is where the wording went when it came off the buttons, so nothing the agent told the person is
  // lost. Declared hidden rather than left out for the same reason as the locations row: whether an
  // agent named its options is a fact about the question, not about the widget.
  Gtk.Label namesLabel {
    use-markup: false;
    wrap: true;
    selectable: true;
    xalign: 0;
    visible: false;
  }

  // The raw input, verbatim, scrollable and capped. `editable: false` + `cursor-visible: false`
  // because this is a thing to read; editable text in an approval dialog invites an edit before an
  // approval, and the answer would then be about a request kurier never received. `max-content-height`
  // is the cap — without it a thousand-line diff makes a dialog whose buttons are below the fold, and
  // `propagate-natural-height` is what lets a short one shrink to its content.
  Gtk.ScrolledWindow {
    max-content-height: 240;
    propagate-natural-height: true;
    hscrollbar-policy: never;
    vscrollbar-policy: automatic;
    child: Gtk.TextView rawInput {
      editable: false;
      cursor-visible: false;
      monospace: true;
      wrap-mode: word;
      left-margin: 8;
      right-margin: 8;
      top-margin: 6;
      bottom-margin: 6;
    };
  }
}